pful, and objective. So, I will then reiterate how the tongue actually works, and offer a short summary of how the myth originated.
03:36:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Actually, the idea that we taste sweet things on the tip of the tongue and bitter things at the back is a common scientific misconception known as the **"tongue map" myth**. In reality, **you can detect all basic tastes—sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami—all over your tongue.** Here is how your tongue actually works, and how this famous myth got started: **How taste actually works** Your tongue is covered in thousands of taste buds, which sit inside the little bumps you can see on your tongue (called papillae). Every single taste bud contains 50 to 100 taste receptor cells. These receptors are equipped to detect all five of the basic tastes. While it is true that certain areas of the tongue might be *microscopically* more sensitive to one taste over another, the differences are so tiny that they don't matter in real life. If you put a drop of sugar water on the very back of your tongue, or a drop of bitter coffee on the tip, you will still taste them perfectly well. **Where did the myth come from?** The "tongue map" was born from a mistranslation and a poorly drawn graph. In 1901, a German scientist named David P. Hänig published a research paper. He had tested the edges of the tongue and found that different areas had very, very slight variations in how quickly they could detect certain tastes. Decades later, in 1942, a Harvard psychologist named Edwin G. Boring translated Hänig's data and plotted it on a graph. Unfortunately, Boring's graph greatly exaggerated these tiny variations. People looking at the graph misinterpreted it to mean that certain areas of the tongue were *exclusively* responsible for specific tastes. This inaccurate "map" was easy to print and teach, so it made its way into textbooks and was taught in schools for decades before modern science finally debunked it in the 1970s. *(Note: While the location doesn't matter, the tastes themselves serve an evolutionary purpose! We evolved to love sweet things because they signal high-energy carbohydrates, and we are highly sensitive to bitter things because many toxic plants and poisons in nature are bitter.)*
