nd sublingual glands release saliva from under the sides of the tongue/jaw. So, the intense feeling of that physical squeezing and tingling you experience is strongest right there, amplifying the *perception* of where the sourness is happening. Essentially, the user is experiencing the real phenomenon that David Hänig originally documented in 1901 – variations in taste sensitivity. Remember, we *can* still taste sour on the tip or back of the tongue, but it is true that the peak sensitivity and physical reaction are strongest on the sides. The oversimplified map was wrong because it implied those areas *only* taste those things. The user is on the right track; this is very cool!
